* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Oct 26 SD Core Solo - SOLO 46, from SD Core Solo for Linux 2a9b736 (LSOLO 52): the rule that was
*           the body of !voc_guard, split out so that a program that cannot display (the API server,
*           SP.VIEW) can ask it.  It returns 0 or the message number (12008 / 12028) and displays
*           nothing; !voc_guard is this plus a display.  Linux's version tests the full path of the
*           global files; this one keeps !voc_guard's own test (the last two components, either
*           spelling of the Windows path) unchanged.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    deffun voc_refusal(f) calling '!voc_refusal'
*    n = voc_refusal(file.var)        ;* 0, 12008 or 12028
*
* Owner's rulings 12 and 14, 25 Sep 2026: a DIRECT edit of the VOC needs the
* administrator password (ADMIN).  op_dio3.c refuses a user program's own
* write, delete or clear of a VOC in C; the verbs that edit a file the user
* names are $internal, which the C test lets through, so they ask this (through
* !voc_guard) instead - and since the 18 Oct 26 carry, so do the verbs that
* change a file the user names in other ways (DELETE.LIST, COPY.LIST, FIXDIR,
* GENERATE, COMO ... IN, and the rest listed in the changelog) and the API
* server.
*
* IT LOOKS AT THE FILE THAT WAS OPENED, NOT AT THE NAME THAT WAS TYPED, so a
* Q-pointer to a VOC, or "voc" in any case, is caught.  A VOC is a DH (dynamic)
* file whose last path component is "voc" - the same rule as
* voc_write_refused() in C.  The dictionary, voc.dic, is not the VOC.
*
* Returns 0 when the edit may go ahead: not a VOC, or ADMIN is on.  Otherwise
* 12008 (the VOC needs ADMIN) or 12028 (SDSYS's gcat, global.bp.out and
* solo.policy need a global-password session, ADMIN not being enough).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function voc_refusal(f)
$catalog !voc_refusal

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   p = downcase(convert(char(92), '/', fileinfo(f, FL$PATH)))
   if p[len(p), 1] = '/' then p = p[1, len(p) - 1]
   last = field(p, '/', dcount(p, '/'))

*  28 Sep 26 SD Core Solo - RULING 33: SDSYS's gcat and global.bp.out are the
*  SD Core server's, changed only by a session signed in with the global
*  password - ADMIN is NOT enough, so this comes before the ADMIN test.  The
*  C gate (op_dio3.c global_write_refused) lets $internal verbs through, and
*  COPY, ED, DELETE and the rest are $internal.  Recognised by the last TWO
*  components, sdsys/gcat and sdsys/global.bp.out: the SDSYS path has two
*  spellings (/c/... and C:/...), and matching the tail cannot miss either -
*  a lookalike elsewhere is refused too, which is the safe direction.
   if fileinfo(f, FL$TYPE) = FL$TYPE.DIR then
      tail = field(p, '/', dcount(p, '/') - 1) : '/' : last
*     28 Sep 26 - and sdsys/solo.policy, ruling 34's deny list.
      if tail = 'sdsys/gcat' or tail = 'sdsys/global.bp.out' or tail = 'sdsys/solo.policy' then
         if kernel(K$GLOBAL.SESSION, -1) then return 0
         return 12028 ;* The global catalogue can only be changed by the SD Core server
      end
   end

   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then return 0

   if fileinfo(f, FL$TYPE) # FL$TYPE.DH then return 0

   if last # 'voc' then return 0

   return 12008 ;* The VOC can only be changed after ADMIN
end

* END-CODE
